GET /api/v1/projects/{project_id}/security/events
Administrative endpoints require elevated permissions.

52. AUTHORIZATION MIDDLEWARE
The API shall implement centralized authorization middleware.
Conceptually:
request
 ↓
authenticate()
 ↓
load_security_context()
 ↓
resolve_project()
 ↓
authorize()
 ↓
domain_handler()
Domain handlers should not rely on each individual route implementing authentication independently.

53. CAPABILITY CHECK INTERFACE
The application should expose a common capability interface.
Conceptually:
authorize(
    actor,
    action,
    resource,
    project
)
The result becomes:
ALLOW
DENY
REQUIRES_APPROVAL
This prevents authorization logic from becoming duplicated throughout the application.

54. POLICY ENGINE
Authorization policies should eventually be represented independently from route implementations.
Conceptually:
POLICY
 ↓
ROLE
 ↓
PERMISSION
 ↓
CAPABILITY
 ↓
RESOURCE
This allows security policy to evolve without rewriting domain services.

55. SECURITY POLICY VERSIONING
Security policies may eventually be versioned.
Each authorization decision may therefore reference:
policy_version
This creates an auditable explanation of which security policy governed a decision.

56. AUTHORIZATION FAILURE
Unauthorized requests return a standard error.
Example:
{
  "error": {
    "code": "AUTHORIZATION_DENIED",
    "message": "The requested operation is not authorized for this project.",
    "resolution": "Contact a project administrator if additional access is required."
  },
  "metadata": {
    "request_id": "uuid",
    "timestamp": "ISO-8601"
  }
}
The response must not disclose protected resource information.

57. AUTHENTICATION FAILURE
Authentication failures use a separate error category.
Example:
AUTHENTICATION_REQUIRED
INVALID_TOKEN
TOKEN_EXPIRED
TOKEN_REVOKED
Authentication and authorization failures must remain distinguishable internally even where external responses are intentionally generic.

58. RATE LIMITING
The API gateway should support rate limiting at multiple levels:
IP
user
organization
project
service
endpoint
Higher-risk endpoints receive stricter controls.
Examples:
authentication
ARIA execution
document generation
large exports
administrative operations

59. ARIA RATE LIMITING
Aria execution shall have independent resource controls.
Limits may be based on:
organization
project
user
command type
compute cost
concurrent jobs
This prevents accidental or malicious command storms from overwhelming the geometry, validation, or documentation workers.

60. JOB AUTHORIZATION
A job inherits authorization context from its originating request.
The job record should contain or reference:
request_id
actor_id
organization_id
project_id
authorization_context
Workers must not re-authorize against stale client input.
They verify the trusted job authorization context and current project state.

61. EVENT AUTHORIZATION
Not every event should be consumable by every service.
Event permissions should distinguish:
publish
consume
administrate
replay
Replay privileges require elevated authorization because replaying an event can cause domain operations to execute again.

62. EVENT REPLAY SECURITY
Event replay must require:
event_id
replay_reason
authorized_actor
target_environment
approval where required
Replay operations are independently audited.

63. ARTIFACT SECURITY
Artifacts shall have:
artifact_id
project_id
revision_id
model_version_id
artifact_type
storage_reference
content_hash
access_policy
The artifact service verifies project authorization before issuing access.

64. DOWNLOAD AUTHORIZATION
A document download request becomes:
REQUEST
 ↓
AUTHENTICATE
 ↓
AUTHORIZE PROJECT
 ↓
AUTHORIZE DOCUMENT
 ↓
VERIFY ARTIFACT
 ↓
ISSUE ACCESS
